// ============================================================================
// DAILY — 2026-09-16 (Wednesday): Today's date as pattern
// ============================================================================
// Weekly rotation slot: the calendar date itself drives the pattern. Today
// (IST) is 2026-09-16 -- day 16, month 09 (September), year 2026.
//
// THE DERIVATION (this is the whole point of the day):
//   The last three Wednesdays each tried a different way of letting the date
//   drive things: one melody reading the digits in sequence (08-26), three
//   voices at three independent clock-rates (09-02), a true note-by-note
//   hocket for the day when day==month (09-09). Today day (16) and month (09)
//   are simply DIFFERENT and roughly coprime-feeling numbers, which suggests
//   a different real device: a genuine POLYMETER. DAY's phrase runs on a
//   16-step grid (its own value, used directly as the grid length); MONTH's
//   phrase runs on a 9-step grid (same). Both grids complete once per Strudel
//   cycle, so the two voices are literally "16 steps against 9 steps" in the
//   same span of time -- a real 16:9 polyrhythm, not a metaphor, generated by
//   nothing but the date's two own numbers.
//
//   DAY's pitches use its own digits (1, 6) as recurring anchors, plus the
//   raw two-digit day number (16) itself dropped in once per phrase as a
//   scale-degree overshoot -- 16 mod 7 = 2, so index 16 lands two octaves
//   plus a major third above tonic, an enormous, easy-to-hear leap that is
//   quite literally "today's date as an interval."
//   MONTH's pitches use its own digits (0, 9): 0 is the tonic anchor, and 9
//   is the same "reach" overshoot 2026-09-09 used for this exact month (9
//   mod 7 = 2, landing an octave + a major third up) -- honest reuse, since
//   September's own digits haven't changed since that file, not a copied
//   arrangement.
//   Both voices cycle through 4 distinct hand-written shapes (call,
//   retrograde-leaning response, compressed-tension, wide-arch resolution)
//   run through Strudel's real `.palindrome()` for a genuine retrograde
//   device on top, rather than just alternating forward statements.
//
//   CYCLES = 25 = day + month (16 + 9). setcpm(75) x 25 cycles / 60 = 20s.
//
// Scale: D major -- deliberately plain (same reasoning as every prior
// Wednesday): the numbers are the idea, not the mode.
//
// NO DRUM KIT, deliberately, not by default: the day/month digits already
// have a real, audible rhythmic outlet -- the two contrasting step-grids
// themselves (16, busy; 9, sparse) -- which IS the piece's polymetric pulse.
// A kick/hat layer on top would just restate that pulse from underneath and
// blur the exact 16-vs-9 contrast that's the point of today's derivation,
// the same failure MUSIC-QC-LOOP.md already flags for reflexive percussion.
//
// Instrumentation: synth basics throughout (square / supersaw / sine), per
// MUSIC-KNOWLEDGE.md §2.5 -- no acoustic-instrument sample names.
//
// This is a DAILY quick clip, not a flagship set -- one continuous pattern,
// no alap/jor/jhala arc.
// ============================================================================

setcpm(75)
let CYCLES = 25 // day + month (16 + 9), the two grid-lengths below

stack(
  // DAY voice -- 16-step grid (day's own value used directly as grid length).
  // Anchors on digits 1 and 6; the raw number 16 drops in once per shape as
  // a two-octave-plus-third overshoot leap -- the date itself, as an interval.
  n("<[1 ~ 6 ~ ~ 3 ~ ~ 16 ~ ~ 6 ~ 1 ~ ~] [~ ~ 1 ~ 3 ~ ~ 6 ~ ~ 16 ~ ~ 6 ~ 1] [~ ~ ~ ~ ~ 1 6 3 ~ 16 ~ 6 1 ~ ~ ~] [6 ~ 16 ~ ~ 3 ~ 1 ~ ~ 6 ~ ~ 1 ~ ~]>")
    .palindrome()
    .scale("D:major")
    .sound("square")
    .attack(.006).decay(.09).sustain(0).release(.04)
    .distort(.18) // acid taste layer, kept light so the polymeter stays legible
    .sometimes(x => x.ply(2)) // <- improv hook: occasional ornament
    .gain(.34)
    .pan(.68)
    .room(.9), // driest voice -- the busier 16-grid reads as the closer, faster one

  // MONTH voice -- 9-step grid. Tonic (0) and the reach-leap (9), same
  // September convention as 09-09, in dialogue with DAY across the stereo
  // field (opposite pan) rather than overlapping it.
  n("<[0 ~ ~ 9 ~ ~ 0 ~ ~] [~ ~ 0 ~ 9 ~ ~ ~ 0] [~ ~ ~ ~ 0 9 ~ ~ 4] [~ ~ ~ 9 ~ ~ ~ ~ 0]>")
    .palindrome()
    .scale("D:major")
    .sound("supersaw")
    .attack(.04).release(.9)
    .lpf(sine.range(500, 2400).slow(16)) // sweep period = day's own value (16)
    .gain(.3)
    .pan(.3)
    .room(2.2), // more spacious -- the sparser 9-grid reads as the wider one

  // YEAR drone (2026 -> digit sum 2+0+2+6=10): a single held tonic/fifth
  // tanpura chord, no pitch movement at all -- but its own pan breathes on a
  // 10-cycle period (the year's digit sum), a self-referential automation
  // this time rather than driving another voice's parameter.
  note("[d2,a2]/25")
    .sound("sine")
    .attack(1.8).release(2.6)
    .pan(sine.range(.35, .65).slow(10))
    .gain(.26)
    .room(5) // most spacious -- year is the slowest, largest unit
)
